From c41620aeb34e0eb0c07d22f8cdead0d223df45d3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 13:18:25 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 28/43] pvr: clamp vkCmdBindVertexBuffers to the binding array
 (silent OOB write)

Same pattern as the vertex-attribute crash fixed in the previous commit: maxVertexInputBindings
is advertised as 16, cmd_buffer->state.vertex_bindings[] has PVR_MAX_VERTEX_INPUT_BINDINGS (16)
entries, and the only guard is an assert() that is compiled out in a release build.

  vb[firstBinding + i] = ...   /* firstBinding + i reaches 31 at bindingCount = 32 */

Unlike the attribute case this does not fault: the overflow lands inside the same
pvr_cmd_buffer_state struct, so it silently corrupts adjacent state. A vbind harness
(vkCmdBindVertexBuffers with 1..32 bindings) therefore reports no crash both before and after -
the defect is out-of-bounds by inspection, not by observation. The loop and the stride call are
now clamped to the array size.

Harness note: the vbind probe must pass the shader's attribute count (16) as its own argument,
separately from the binding count under test, otherwise the previous commit's attribute guard
rejects the pipeline first.

No regressions: bda, vk13, pctest, vk16, wgsize, samplers, storageimages, mrt, varyings, ubos,
stgbuf, vattrib(16) all PASS; glmark2 --validate 27/0.
---
 src/imagination/vulkan/pvr_arch_cmd_buffer.c | 14 ++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/imagination/vulkan/pvr_arch_cmd_buffer.c b/src/imagination/vulkan/pvr_arch_cmd_buffer.c
index 4ada520..b01039a 100644
--- a/src/imagination/vulkan/pvr_arch_cmd_buffer.c
+++ b/src/imagination/vulkan/pvr_arch_cmd_buffer.c
@@ -3093,7 +3093,17 @@ void PVR_PER_ARCH(CmdBindVertexBuffers2)(VkCommandBuffer commandBuffer,
 
    PVR_CHECK_COMMAND_BUFFER_BUILDING_STATE(cmd_buffer);
 
-   for (uint32_t i = 0; i < bindingCount; i++) {
+   /* vertex_bindings[] has PVR_MAX_VERTEX_INPUT_BINDINGS (16) entries and the assert above
+    * is compiled out in a release build, so an over-limit vkCmdBindVertexBuffers wrote past
+    * the end of the array - silently corrupting the rest of pvr_cmd_buffer_state rather than
+    * faulting, which is why it is easy to miss. maxVertexInputBindings is advertised as 16;
+    * clamp to the array instead of writing out of bounds. */
+   const uint32_t bind_count =
+      MIN2(bindingCount,
+           (uint32_t)(PVR_MAX_VERTEX_INPUT_BINDINGS - MIN2(firstBinding,
+                                                           (uint32_t)PVR_MAX_VERTEX_INPUT_BINDINGS)));
+
+   for (uint32_t i = 0; i < bind_count; i++) {
       VK_FROM_HANDLE(pvr_buffer, buffer, pBuffers[i]);
       const uint64_t size = pSizes ? pSizes[i] : VK_WHOLE_SIZE;
 
@@ -3107,7 +3117,7 @@ void PVR_PER_ARCH(CmdBindVertexBuffers2)(VkCommandBuffer commandBuffer,
    if (pStrides != NULL) {
       vk_cmd_set_vertex_binding_strides(&cmd_buffer->vk,
                                         firstBinding,
-                                        bindingCount,
+                                        bind_count,
                                         pStrides);
    }
 
